// pkg/postgres/composite.bit — json/jsonb and array identifiers (#5452,
// epic #5449), sibling of `pkg/postgres/temporal.bit`/`scalar.bit`
// (#5450/#5451): same correction, same driver, but a smaller file than the
// ticket assumed.
//
// TEXT, NOT BINARY, SAME AS ITS SIBLINGS (epic #5449, comment 1).
// `query.bit`'s `bindFrame` asks for `fmtText` on every result column, so a
// json/jsonb/array value never carries the binary wire shapes the original
// ticket described (jsonb's one-byte version header, the binary array
// header with its ndim/flags/element-OID). Every one of those columns
// arrives as Postgres's own text rendering: jsonb/json as the document
// text, an array as `{1,2,3}` (`{}` empty, a bare `NULL` token for a null
// element, a quoted element for one containing a comma/brace/quote/
// backslash).
//
// NO NEW DECODE PATH HERE — THE GENERIC ACCESSOR ALREADY DOES THE WORK.
// `conn.bit`'s `valueForColumn` has no json/jsonb/array case, so those
// columns fall through to `Value.Text` unconditionally (its own "unmapped
// OID" fallback), which is exactly what `std/sql`'s `sqlReqJson`/
// `sqlReqJsonAs`/`sqlOptJson`/`sqlOptJsonAs` and `sqlReqArray`/
// `sqlOptArray` plus the `sqlElemInt`/`sqlElemFloat`/`sqlElemBool`/
// `sqlElemText`/`sqlElemBlob`/`sqlElemDecimal` element decoders
// (`stdlib/sql/rowtypes.bit`, landed by #5457) already parse end to end —
// including the NULL-element and quoting traps this ticket named. Writing
// a second Postgres-local json/array decoder here would duplicate that
// work byte for byte (the ticket's own instruction: report the smaller
// scope rather than pad it). `composite.test.bit` proves the specific
// acceptance cases `pkg/postgres/row.test.bit` (#5457) does not yet cover:
// byte-identical jsonb/json round trips, an empty array, a single-element
// array, a NULL array element, and a text array element containing a
// comma and a brace.
//
// WHAT IS LEFT: THE OIDS. Nothing in this package identifies a json or
// jsonb column by its wire OID (`codec.bit` and `scalar.bit` each declare
// the OIDs their own types need — `oidInet`/`oidTimestamp` in `codec.bit`
// are the same "declared, not necessarily decoded here" shape). Arrays get
// no OID constant: Postgres has one array OID per element type
// (`_int4` = 1007, `_text` = 1009, ...) and nothing in this package
// dispatches on it — `sqlReqArray<T>` takes its element decoder from the
// caller, not from a lookup table — so adding an enumeration of array OIDs
// here would be unused surface, not a codec.

// `json` (`pg_type.typname = 'json'`) and `jsonb` (`'jsonb'`). Both decode
// identically on this driver's text-only path: `json` preserves the
// document's original text (whitespace, key order); `jsonb` normalizes
// both server-side before ever reaching the wire. Neither difference is
// this file's to handle — it is Postgres's own rendering, already done by
// the time `Value.Text` reaches `sqlReqJson`.
export const oidJson: int = 114
export const oidJsonb: int = 3802
